## 11. Real AI Integration

Use Gemini for the initial AI-powered features.

Use the Google AI Studio environment's supported Gemini integration.

Model requests must be processed through an appropriate server-side implementation.

Never put a private API key directly in frontend source code, browser localStorage, or a client-side request.

Do not require the user to paste an API key into a public-facing interface.

### AI behavior

Use structured responses where appropriate.

For the What-If Engine, return data in a predictable structure containing branch titles, descriptions, consequences, affected characters, and possible contradictions.

Validate the AI response before displaying it.

If the response is malformed, handle the error gracefully.

Do not fabricate results when an API request fails.

Display meaningful loading, empty, error, and retry states.

### Context management

Construct prompts from approved canon, relevant character profiles, and the current user request.

Keep experimental branches separate unless the user explicitly chooses to include them.

If the project is too large to send its complete context, clearly indicate that the AI used a limited subset of the story information.

Do not silently claim perfect memory of an entire novel.

### Model independence

Design the AI integration so it can be replaced or extended with another authorized provider in the future.

Do not build additional provider integrations in the initial version.

Do not claim to bypass model safety policies or guarantee support for every type of generated content.

